   "source": "**Reading this first look** (2002 files only: origination 50,000 loans by 31 columns, performance 2,497,266 rows by 35 columns)\n\n**Size**\n- One year of the performance table takes about 750 MB of memory once loaded. Loading all six years in full would need several gigabytes, which is why the later notebooks read one year at a time and only the columns they need.\n- The last rows of the performance table are from June 2026, so that is where the data end.\n\n**Origination table**\n- `isnull().sum()` finds only three columns with blank cells: column 4 (a metropolitan area code, blank for 26,832 of the 50,000 loans in 2002) and columns 25 and 26 (blank in every row). Everything else looks complete, **but that is misleading**: the minimum and maximum in `describe()` show hidden \"not available\" codes. Credit score goes up to 9999, columns 5, 8, 9 and 11 go up to 999, and columns 6 and 22 go up to 99. These are codes, not real values: the user guide confirms that they mean \"Not Available\".\n- The postal code (column 18) is stored as a number, so leading zeros are lost (for example a New Hampshire code that starts with 0 shows as 32). It must be read as text if I ever use it.\n- Columns 28 and 30 have a spread of exactly 0: every loan has the same value (7 and 9999), so they carry no information.\n- The largest first payment date (column 1) is February 2009 for a loan in the 2002 file. That is odd, and it is the same kind of case as the few late first payments in notebook 01b.\n\n**Performance table**\n- Many columns are blank in almost every row. Columns 13 to 21 have values in only 600 of 2.5 million rows, and several others in only a few thousand. They are probably only filled in for special events such as a modification or a property sale. I do not use them.\n- Column 8 (the zero balance code) has a value in 49,711 rows. That equals the number of loans that have ended, so 289 of the 50,000 loans were still active in June 2026.\n- **A second hidden code:** column 25 equals 999 in 95% of all rows over the six years (98% in 2002). The other values are small numbers (10 to 14 and so on). The user guide gives 999 as \"Not Available\", and by its attribute order this column is the estimated loan-to-value.\n- A few extreme values, which I checked across all six years: a current interest rate above 15% appears in only 7 rows from 4 loans (the highest is 40%, in a loan with a modification flag), and the remaining months are negative in only 276 of 20.7 million rows. These are tiny and probably not errors that matter, but I should not forget them.\n- The delinquency status (column 3) is read as text, because it contains `RA`."
