   "source": "## 5. What I see\n\n**The files**\n- Six years, 50,000 loans each (300,000 in total), all with unique loan identifiers. The origination files have 31 columns and the performance files 35, the same for every year.\n\n**Data quality**\n- Missing values are hidden as codes: credit score `9999` (about 0.2% of loans), debt-to-income `999` (about 2.2% of loans) and, very rarely, `999` in loan-to-value. A normal missing-value check shows 0% missing, which is misleading. I treat these codes as unknown. The user guide confirms that these codes mean \"Not Available\".\n\n**Did the borrowers change?** (middle values by year)\n- Credit scores stay steady (about 729 to 742).\n- Loan-to-value(LTV) and debt-to-income(DTI) creep up: loan-to-value goes from 70 (2003) to 77 (2006 and 2007), and debt-to-income from 30 to 37. Loans also get bigger (about 129,000 to 160,000).\n- The interest rate follows the market (5.5% in 2003, 6.38% in 2006 and 2007), so it says more about the economy than about the borrowers.\n- So the inputs shift a little towards riskier terms, but not dramatically. This is one reason the defaults rise (see notebook 02), but I cannot say it is the only reason.\n\n**The performance files**\n- Later loan years are watched for fewer months on average (about 60 months for 2007 against about 82 for 2003), because the data stop at a fixed date.\n- The share of loan-months that were late rises from about 2% to 9%, in line with the default rates in notebook 02.\n\n**Still to do**\n- The user guide confirms the column names (see `src/columns.py`) and that `9999` and `999` mean \"Not Available\".\n- Still missing: the separate technical file layout (field types and positions), which I have not downloaded."
